@page "/scroll"
@inject BmotionScrollTracker Scroll
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<PageTitle>Scroll · Bmotion samples</PageTitle>

<h1>Scroll</h1>

<p>
    The bar along the top of every page is the page's scroll, played by the browser on its own scroll timeline
    (<code>Timeline="BmScrollTimeline.Page()"</code>). Its keyframes are worked out in C# once and handed over; after
    that there is no scroll handler and no interop, so it follows the scroll as closely on Server and in the MAUI app as
    on WebAssembly. Scroll down.
</p>

<div class="scroll-readout" aria-live="off">
    @* The scroll position as a number in C#, for when it is needed there rather than on an element. Every scroll
       event crosses to .NET - over the circuit on Server - and the count says how many have. *@
    <span>BmotionScrollTracker: <strong id="scroll-progress">@_progress.ToString("P0", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</strong> scrolled</span>
    <span class="muted"><span id="scroll-callbacks">@_callbacks</span> callbacks</span>
</div>

<section>
    <h2>Each card's own journey</h2>
    <p>
        <code>BmScrollTimeline.View()</code> measures the element's journey through the viewport instead of the page's:
        0 as it enters at the bottom, 1 as it leaves at the top. These cards fade and grow in on the way up, and back
        out as they go. Only transforms and opacity can ride a scroll timeline - they are what the browser
        interpolates by itself.
    </p>

    <div class="journey">
        @foreach (var card in Enumerable.Range(1, 5))
        {
            <Bmotion Timeline="BmScrollTimeline.View()" Animate="Bm.To(opacity: [0, 1, 1, 0], scale: [0.85, 1, 1, 0.85])">
                <div class="journey-card">Card @card</div>
            </Bmotion>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Triggered by the viewport</h2>
    <p>
        <code>WhileInView</code> is a gesture rather than a timeline: the browser reports the element entering the
        viewport, and it plays an ordinary animation from there. <code>BmPresets.Reveal()</code> packages the usual one,
        once per element.
    </p>

    <ul class="reveals">
        @foreach (var (index, text) in Reveals.Index())
        {
            <Bmotion @key="index" Preset="index % 2 == 0 ? RevealFromLeft : RevealFromRight">
                <li class="reveal">@text</li>
            </Bmotion>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

@code {
    private static readonly BmPreset RevealFromLeft = BmPresets.Reveal(from: BmEdge.Left);
    private static readonly BmPreset RevealFromRight = BmPresets.Reveal(from: BmEdge.Right);

    private static readonly string[] Reveals =
    [
        "Each line waits off its place until the viewport reaches it.",
        "Each line plays once, the first time it enters.",
        "Scrolling back up leaves them where they are.",
        "With reduced motion they fade in without moving.",
    ];

    private double _progress;
    private int _callbacks;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender is false) return;

        await Scroll.ObserveAsync(info =>
        {
            _progress = info.ProgressY;
            _callbacks++;
            return InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        });
    }

    // The tracker is transient: this page owns it, and stops observing by disposing it.
    public ValueTask DisposeAsync() => Scroll.DisposeAsync();
}
